# The default track policy for a .jcodebuddy/ tree: everything in here is derived, machine-written
# and machine-local, so nothing is committed unless a project says otherwise. The one reserved
# exception is conf/, which holds what MUST survive a clone and a commit.
#
# The palette of subfolders is deliberately open — projects are still inventing them (metadata/,
# index/, cache/, reports/, agent-state/, webview/, …) — so a tool does not have to be listed here
# to be ignored. It has to be listed to be *tracked*.
*
!.gitignore
!README.md

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This module's opt-ins. It keeps the *explanation* of every subtree in git — the README.md that
# says what the subtree is — and none of its content, plus one hand-written spec in context/.
#
# The directory must be re-included before anything inside it: git never re-includes a file whose
# parent directory is excluded. That is why each of these is two lines (or, for a subtree whose
# READMEs are nested, a line per level).
!context/
!context/**
!reports/
!reports/README.md

!metadata/
!metadata/README.md
!metadata/*/
!metadata/*/README.md

!index/
!index/README.md

# A subtree that is opted in for its *content* rather than its explanation needs the same two-line
# shape, and must stay narrow enough not to carry generated .java: a .java under .jcodebuddy/ is
# always a mistake (DEC-026 section 5, and GeneratorGuardTest asserts it).
#
#     !metadata/entity/
#     !metadata/entity/**
#     metadata/entity/**/*.java

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reserved. conf/ is the one subtree that is tracked by default: it holds what must be preserved
# across a clone and a commit — the settings a project wants its contributors and its tools to
# share. Everything else is opt-in, and a project that adds a subfolder does not have to come back
# here to have it ignored.
!conf/
!conf/**
